N2  - Abstract This chapter thoroughly explores aerial radio access networks (ARANs), delving into technological intricacies, propagation models, and airborne connectivity challenges. Beginning with aerial radio base stations and antennas, it uncovers foundational components shaping ARANs. Investigating the capacity realm, it scrutinizes the dynamic landscape across low-altitude platforms (LAPs), high-altitude platforms (HAPs), and medium-altitude platforms (MAPs), spanning from low Earth orbits (LEOs) to geostationary Earth orbits (GEOs). The connectivity, architecture, and services domain of ARANs are dissected, providing insights into pivotal elements defining operational frameworks. Propagation channel models, including Friis and specialized models for diverse scenarios, are explored. Navigating ARAN challenges, the chapter addresses the intricacies of reliable devices, scalability, density, and backhaul connectivity. Emphasizing power management, it scrutinizes issues of interference, coexistence, and regulatory landscapes. Highlighting mobility's significance, the chapter stresses robust, scalable solutions balancing performance with safety, viewing the ascent into the skies not just as a technological feat but as a socially responsible pursuit, considering regulatory and privacy aspects governing airspace.
